{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And he shall be shaved, but the neteq he shall not shave</b> — According to its plain sense, it warns that he must not pass a razor over the place of the neteq, for even though there is no hair in it, if he passes the razor over that place the skin will be scraped and it will cause hair to grow there, for so is the way of those who have boils on the head and all those whose hair has fallen out — to scrape the place and also to make incised cuts upon it with a blade. But in Torat Kohanim (9:7) they interpreted: \"But the neteq he shall not shave\" — and what is there in it [to shave]? That is to say, the hair has already been plucked from it. Rather, [it means] adjacent to the neteq he shall not shave. How so? He shaves outside it and leaves two hairs adjacent to the neteq, so that it will be recognizable if it spreads. And this is what Onkelos translated (Onkelos on Leviticus 13:33): \"וְיִגַלַח סַחְרָנֵי נִתְקָא וּדְעִם נִתְקָא לָא יְגַלַח\" — that he should leave in all its surroundings two hairs, to recognize the spreading by them. And the sense of \"אֶת\" will be like \"וַיִּגַּשׁ דָּוִד אֶת הָעָם וַיִּשְׁאַל לָהֶם לְשָׁלוֹם\" (1 Samuel 30:21), where [David] came adjacent to them and did not come among them. Or its sense is like the sense of \"עִם\" (with/next to), and the word \"אֲשֶׁר\" is elided, so it says: and that which is with the neteq he shall not shave, and there are many like it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:36.552968358Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}